--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -4,6 +4,8 @@ Each release's page on GitHub has its notes and measurements. See [the 1.0.0 rel
 
 ## Unreleased
 
+- Opt-in: `TENSORFOLD_PREFILL_VISIT_MS=N` ends a prompt fill pass once it has run N ms while a stream could decode, so decoding streams wait at most about N ms plus one prompt call for their next round when many short prompts arrive together. `TENSORFOLD_PREFILL_SHARE_SHORT=1` makes `TENSORFOLD_PREFILL_SHARE` bill those short-prompt bursts as it bills long prompt steps. Neither cuts a prompt into different calls, so replies are the same tokens; unset, nothing changes.
+
 - On Ryzen AI Max (RDNA 3.5) GPUs, one- and two-row decode launches use narrower stream tiles, so Qwen3.6-35B-A3B chat decodes about 18% faster with drafts on, and Qwen3.8-27B about 5% faster, with the same tokens (#1855). Thanks to @AbrahamCete.
 
 - Flash Next on RDNA 3.5 iGPUs (gfx1150 to gfx1153) fills prompts in 4,096-row chunks by default on one GPU, falling back to 1,024 when the larger chunk would admit fewer streams. Tokens are unchanged (#1651, fixes #1649). Thanks to @gilby.
diff --git a/RUNBOOK.md b/RUNBOOK.md
index bd24f69263780d2245eef72576668c587e28408b..d0eec6c00d435f20d9f0b585ef960cb0dd396c08 100644
--- a/RUNBOOK.md
+++ b/RUNBOOK.md
@@ -136,7 +136,7 @@ When API authentication is enabled, `/health` returns only status.
 
 ## Prompt prefill share, budget and admission policy
 
-The native server reads four environment variables when it opens its lane host.
+The native server reads six environment variables when it opens its lane host.
 All are optional. Set them before starting the server.
 `TENSORFOLD_PREFILL_SHARE` takes a number from 0.01 to 1.
 The default is 1. Any other text gives the default.
@@ -165,6 +165,20 @@ Lowering it gives those streams their next round sooner.
 That costs more passes to finish the prompts.
 With the default 0 no cap applies: short prompts run as one burst beside decoders, bounded at a lane's worth of newly begun prompts while other work waits.
 
+`TENSORFOLD_PREFILL_VISIT_MS` takes a whole number of milliseconds from 1 to 4,294,967,295.
+The default is 0, which means none; any other text gives 0.
+It is the row budget's wall-time form: once a pass of prompt work has run that long while a stream is ready to decode, the host starts no further prompt call in that pass and the decoders take their round.
+The rest of the prompt work continues on the next pass.
+A prompt call is never cut, so each prompt is filled in exactly the calls it would be without the budget, and a pass can run past the budget by one call.
+Every pass makes at least one call, and, as with the row budget, a long prompt that has waited long enough goes first, so short arrivals cannot starve it.
+Unlike the row budget it keeps the same pause length when rows cost more, for example on a longer cached prefix.
+
+`TENSORFOLD_PREFILL_SHARE_SHORT=1` bills short prompts to `TENSORFOLD_PREFILL_SHARE` too.
+Without it (the default, or any value but `1`) only long prompt steps owe decoders time, as described above.
+With it and a share below 1, a burst of short prompts run beside decoding streams owes them its time at the same rate as a long step, the next long step waits on that debt, and the next short burst waits until what earlier bursts owe is paid.
+A long step's debt never holds a short prompt back.
+At share 1 it changes nothing.
+
 `TENSORFOLD_PREFILL_POLICY` takes `fcfs`.
 Unset, or any other text, keeps the default order described above.
 Under `fcfs` the host fills only the oldest long prompt (more than 512 uncached rows) on each visit, in chunks of `TENSORFOLD_PREFILL_TOKENS` rows, and the decoding streams take one round after each chunk.
